            <ArticleTitle>Hypersensitivity pneumonitis resulting from community exposure to Canada goose droppings: when an external environmental antigen becomes an indoor environmental antigen.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>84-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">In the past, hypersensitivity pneumonitis has been attributed to occupational, agricultural, or home environmental exposure.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">This report describes the first case of hypersensitivity pneumonitis due to community exposure to droppings from Canada geese migrating through a suburban environment.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">Clinical and serologic information was used in making the diagnosis of hypersensitivity pneumonitis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Serologic analysis demonstrated precipitating antibodies against goose droppings and against an extract made from washings from a filter taken from the patient's office. These studies also showed that the antigens in the office filter were goose dropping antigens.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Hypersensitivity pneumonitis can result from exposure to goose dropping antigens in the community that enter buildings through ventilation systems. This represents a new form of an old disease.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
